"/bin/zsh -lc 'review_schema=$(mktemp /tmp/rota-review-schema.XXXXXX)\nprintf %s '\"'{\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\",\\\"properties\\\":{\\\"verdict\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\"},\\\"findings\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"array\\\",\\\"items\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\"}}},\\\"required\\\":[\\\"verdict\\\",\\\"findings\\\"],\\\"additionalProperties\\\":false}' > \\\"\"'$review_schema\"\ncodex exec --sandbox read-only --skip-git-repo-check --ephemeral --output-schema \"$review_schema\" '\"'Review this Storybloq setup package read-only. Do not write files or run tests/install/build. Read README.md, pyproject.toml, rota/schedule.py and tests/test_schedule.py to verify evidence.\nSources: README first version CSV volunteers/unavailable days, fair weekly shifts (max count minus min <=1), print rota; email later. User: one food-bank organiser laptop, whole-day unavailability, if fair impossible print fairest possible and who is over, keep Python, use judgment for other choices.\nProposed choices: volunteers CSV columns volunteer,unavailable_days with semicolon-separated ISO dates; unique nonblank volunteer names for v1. shifts CSV shift_id,date, one row means one single-volunteer shift; unique nonblank IDs, ISO dates all within chosen Monday-Sunday week (--week YYYY-MM-DD Monday). Multiple shifts per day allowed, no extra per-day limit beyond availability (explicit assumption). Missing availability means available, empty volunteers rejected; zero shifts prints empty rota. Validate malformed CSV, duplicates, dates, missing fields and file errors with actionable diagnostics before assignment. No external services or new technology commitment.\nOptimization: never assign on unavailable day; maximize filled shifts, then minimize max-minus-min assignment counts over ALL input volunteers including zero counts; among ties minimize sum of squared counts then deterministic stable ID tie-break. Report any unfilled shift with reason and all workload counts. If count gap exceeds 1, list every volunteer above min(counts)+1 and excess over that threshold. This is weekly count fairness only, not hours/history. Algorithm must demonstrate exact optimum, not claim greedy is optimal.\nPlan phases: p0 existing baseline, p1 input workflow, p2 scheduling correctness, p3 usable CLI and acceptance. Proposed tickets (labels temporary):\nB baseline complete: round-robin assign and ValueError empty volunteers established by source, one test source present but NOT run.\nI CSV validation (p1): outcome validated volunteers and shifts; scope schemas/week rules above; excludes scheduling; acceptance valid records parsed, invalid rejected; behavior missing files/errors, empty shift success and empty volunteers error; verification pytest table cases; prerequisites none; assumptions schemas as above.\nC1 Checkpoint sample inputs (p1), blocked I: owner reviews synthetic sample CSVs and parsed week; question is whether input workflow fits weekly use.\nS exact availability-aware fairest assignment (p2), blocked I: complete objective and over threshold above; excludes hours/history/emails; acceptance availability, maximal fill, global fairness; behavior impossible fairness produces best effort, empty cases explicit; verification exhaustive small-case oracle comparisons, impossible fairness and zero-count fixtures, repeated-run determinism; prerequisites I; assumptions equal shift weight/multiple shifts per day allowed.\nC2 Checkpoint fairness examples (p2), blocked S: owner reviews balanced, unavoidable overload, unfilled sample outputs and accepts definition.\nO CLI/reporting (p3), blocked S,C1,C2: python -m rota --volunteers file --shifts file --week Monday; readable plain-text dated rota plus all workload counts, unfilled shifts and overload list. --help documents columns, objective, threshold and exit status: 0 complete fair, 2 input error, 3 valid best-effort output with gaps or unfairness; stderr input errors; no partial rota on invalid input. No color-only meaning. Verification subprocess pytest tests for all statuses, both report warnings together and docs example; excludes email, UI/server; no prerequisites besides listed.\nC3 Checkpoint first-version acceptance (p3), blocked O: organiser demonstrates README and user outcomes on synthetic representative weekly data.\n7 tickets total (B,I,C1,S,C2,O,C3), 4 phases including default p0. Existing code counts only B complete; no full scheduler or CSV capability claimed.\nCoverage: README CSV->I,C1; availability/fairness->S,C2; printing->O,C3; user laptop Python->O; best effort/over->S,O; email explicit deferred. No GitHub import requested.\nGovernance: new AGENTS.md purpose/scope/Python3.11/modules/testing/TBD-none; RULES.md hard availability, lexicographic fairness objective, explicit reporting, TDD business logic, synthetic data; REVIEW.md skill template verbatim. No existing governance files. Git init and ignore .story/snapshots/, .story/sessions/, .story/status.json, __pycache__/, .pytest_cache/, .venv/, *.pyc. No implementation code changes.\nQuality Full pipeline because scheduling business rules; WRITE_TESTS and TEST enabled command python -m pytest established by pytest optional dependency/config. BUILD disabled no established build target (pending: packaging build if distribution needed, outside v1); VERIFY disabled CLI not server, CLI behavior through tests. No npm commands, installs or test/build execution during setup. Initial snapshot, setup note, handover capture decisions coverage and review. Checkpoints ordinary tickets, not enforced gates.\nAssess fidelity/coverage, usability, dependencies, correctness/fairness, verification and risks. Return verdict and concrete findings.'\""
